window.docContents[179].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: Module.length\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6/Derived2; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: Order.LTSeries.length_le_krullDim\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6/Derived2; role=supporting\n\nThe auxiliary function algebra, viewed as a module over itself, satisfies the displayed imported module predicate.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.5; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary function module satisfies the displayed imported module predicate.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.5; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nThere is no linear equivalence from the auxiliary function algebra to the auxiliary function module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.5; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nThe product of two copies of the auxiliary function algebra is linearly equivalent to the product of two copies of the auxiliary function module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.5; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nDeclaration: isFiniteLength_iff_isNoetherian_isArtinian\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.8.6/Derived2; role=supporting\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length\u0009Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-the-quaternion-group-Q_8/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--Irreducible-representations-of-the-quaternion-group-Q_8":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples","header":"Irreducible representations of the quaternion group Q_8","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-the-quaternion-group-Q_8/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--Irreducible-representations-of-the-quaternion-group-Q_8"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Section-5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___17___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.17. The hook length formula\u0009Section 5.17: The hook length formula\u0009Section 5.17: The hook length formula","header":"5.17. The hook length formula","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Section-5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___17___heading-1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2/#Chapter6___Example6___2___3":{"contents":"Example 6.2.3 (A_2). The quiver A_2 consists of two vertices connected by a single edge:\n\n\\bullet \\xrightarrow{} \\bullet\nA representation of this quiver consists of two vector spaces V, W and an operator A : V \\to W:\n\n\n\\overset{V}{\\bullet} \\xrightarrow{A} \\overset{W}{\\bullet}.\n\n\nTo decompose this representation, we first let V' be a complement to the kernel of A in V and let W' be a complement to the image of A in W. Then we can decompose the representation as follows:\n\n\n\\overset{V}{\\bullet} \\xrightarrow{A} \\overset{W}{\\bullet} = \\overset{\\ker A}{\\bullet} \\xrightarrow{0} \\overset{0}{\\bullet} \\oplus \\overset{V'}{\\bullet} \\xrightarrow[\\sim]{A} \\overset{\\operatorname{Im} A}{\\bullet} \\oplus \\overset{0}{\\bullet} \\xrightarrow{0} \\overset{W'}{\\bullet}.\n\n\nThe first summand is a multiple of the object 1 \\to 0, the second a multiple of 1 \\xrightarrow{\\sim} 1, and the third of 0 \\to 1. We see that the quiver A_2 has three indecomposable representations, namely\n\n\n1 \\to 0, \\quad 1 \\xrightarrow{\\sim} 1, \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 0 \\to 1.\n\n\nNote that this statement is just the Gauss elimination theorem for matrices.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$\u0009Example 6.2.3: Indecomposable representations of A2","header":"Example 6.2.3: Indecomposable representations of A2","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___3___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A2/#Chapter6___Example6___2___3"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Definition-6___4___1___-Cartan-matrix/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Definition-6___4___1___-Cartan-matrix--Definition-6___4___1___-Cartan-matrix--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary transformation of square integer matrices indexed by a finite type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Definition6.4.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots\u0009Definition 6.4.1: Cartan matrix\u0009Definition 6.4.1: Cartan matrix\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Definition-6___4___1___-Cartan-matrix/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Definition-6___4___1___-Cartan-matrix--Definition-6___4___1___-Cartan-matrix--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p/#Chapter6___Theorem6___8___1":{"contents":"Theorem 6.8.1. There exists m \\in \\mathbb{N}, such that\n\nd\\left(V^{(m)}\\right) = \\alpha_p\n\nfor some p.\n\nProof. If V^{(i)} is surjective at the appropriate vertex k, then\n\nd\\left(V^{(i+1)}\\right) = d\\left(F_k^+ V^{(i)}\\right) = s_k d\\left(V^{(i)}\\right).\n\nThis implies that if V^{(0)}, \\ldots, V^{(i-1)} are surjective at the appropriate vertices, then\n\nd\\left(V^{(i)}\\right) = \\ldots s_{n-1} s_n d(V).\n\nBy Lemma 6.7.2 this cannot continue indefinitely, since d\\left(V^{(i)}\\right) may not have any negative entries. Let i be the smallest number such that V^{(i)} is not surjective at the appropriate vertex. By Proposition 6.6.7 it is indecomposable. So, by Proposition 6.6.5, we get\n\nd(V^{(i)}) = \\alpha_p\n\nfor some p. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Theorem 6.8.1: Dimension vector eventually becomes alpha_p","header":"Theorem 6.8.1: Dimension vector eventually becomes alpha_p","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Theorem-6___8___1___-Dimension-vector-eventually-becomes-alpha_p/#Chapter6___Theorem6___8___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.1. Lifting of idempotents","header":"Lifting complete systems of orthogonal idempotents","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents"}});